A round is a budget, and three bugs that only appear once it runs

**#5 was merged mid-flight and these six commits landed after.** Nothing here is new work
since — it is the second half of that branch, cut fresh from `main` so it is independently
mergeable. It has been running locally against `constellation.anecdote.channel` and
`cite-fort-collins` all afternoon; four seats have spoken.

Three of the four commits are bugs found by **running** it, and one is bad enough that I would
not leave `main` sitting on it.

## The three bugs

**A seat with no `ready` items killed the round.** `grep` exits 1 when it matches nothing, and
zero `status: ready` is the *normal* case — `draft` is where things are supposed to sit. Under
`set -o pipefail` that exit killed the script **between `git add -A` and the commit**, so a
session that had genuinely been done was staged and then discarded.

Its shape is why it matters more than its size: **it is indistinguishable from the agent having
done nothing.** The branch shows the previous quiet session, the work order is gone because the
agent deleted it, and the seat reads as up to date having said nothing at all. I only caught it
by holding the agent's own summary of what it had written next to a branch that did not contain
it. It is in `run.sh` too, where it fires on the hosted path for exactly the same reason; fixed
in both.

**`worktree add --force` overrides a busy path, not a busy branch.** Git refuses to
force-update a ref another worktree has checked out and no flag turns that off. So a single
leftover worktree — from an interrupted run, or from the workspace location moving — **retires
a seat permanently**: it can never be checked out again until a person finds the registration by
hand. Every worktree of the repo holding the seat's branch is released first now.

**A stale registration could do the same from the other side**, and `session.mjs` threw rather
than continuing, which stranded every seat behind it in the loop. Prune unconditionally; a
leftover is not a reason to abort a session.

## A round is a budget, not a sweep

The merged version runs every seat in every repo every time the clock fires. That is nine seats
across four repositories here, and a session is **minutes of real attention** — an hour per
round, which is exactly how a cadence stops being affordable and then stops being kept at all.

`--max` serves the **stalest** seats and the rest wait. **Waiting costs nothing**: a range is
cumulative, so a seat served three rounds from now reads everything it would have read today.
Seats carrying a standing work order go first, because they were already interrupted once.

Selection is **read-only**, which is load-bearing rather than tidy: the mechanical half advances
the pin, so preparing every seat in order to choose one would spend the range of every seat that
did not get chosen.

## The report is a hub, because an index only moved the problem

The merged version writes one index page. In use that was half an answer — it tells you which
seats exist and then sends you to a branch per seat to read a finding. **A hub you have to leave
in order to learn anything is not a hub.**

So the report branch carries `README.md` **and a page per seat holding that seat's actual
documents** — position, complaints, asks, last session note — copied whole from the branch, which
stays the authority. `/tree/council` renders the index with everything one click away, **and
nothing has to be turned on**. Pages for retired seats are deleted rather than left looking
current.

The same pages render into a wiki with `--as wiki` (`Home`, `_Sidebar`, `Seat-<name>`) for anyone
who has opened one; the difference is only how GitHub addresses them.

## Verified

- `npm test` passes. The code here is byte-identical to what ran this afternoon.
- Four real sessions, committed and pushed by the loop:
  - **`consent`** reported all three of its goals `unmeasured` rather than estimating, and ruled
    the range's merged PR out of scope on grounds its own config gives.
  - **`presentation`** filed two `open` complaints against this node's stated goals: `BOUNDARY.md`
    landed at the root with nothing on the visible side pointing at it, and `README.md` still says
    *"Two, both `session: local`"* while `advocate.yml` has carried three seats since that range
    merged.
  - **`contributor`** (cite-fort-collins) found that `_intermediates/`, the artifact handed
    wholesale to NCCV, holds a cited piece's full prose with no mechanism to prune it when a
    citation is withdrawn — and corrected a prior "quiet session" commit that had wrongly claimed
    nothing to report. That commit was the pipefail bug's own footprint.
- Budget and rotation observed across three rounds: two seats served and one left waiting; the
  next round rotated to the other repo and served exactly one.
- Hubs live at
  [`constellation/tree/council`](https://github.com/FCCN-ANTIBODY/constellation.anecdote.channel/tree/council)
  and `cite-fort-collins/tree/council`.

## Still true from #5, and still not acted on

- **`run.sh` advances the pin at prepare time.** If the judgement half never happens, the range is
  recoverable only from the JSON inside `sessions/PENDING.md`. Losing the order loses the range,
  silently. Presumably deliberate; worth saying out loud.
- **The `advocate/` namespace holds two kinds of thing.** `advocate/seat-addressing` was a feature
  branch and is now merged; `advocate/addressing` is the seat's workspace. Nothing breaks, but both
  `pending.mjs` and `digest.mjs` find seats by branch name.
- **No test for `round.sh` or `publish.sh`.** Shell over git and an agent; testing them honestly
  needs a scratch repo and a fake agent. Worth doing, not done here.

